00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.830
Lukas, what would you
say is the hottest

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:34.660
thing that you have
to manage, in terms

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:36.608
of managing your GCP cost?

00:02:36.608 --> 00:02:38.150
LUKAS KARLSSON:
Well, as I mentioned,

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:41.020
we have a large number of
different billing accounts.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.790
So when we get our
bill each month,

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:45.550
we have to sort of split
it up and charge back

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:50.020
to 300 different internal cost
objects, which each represent

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.120
a different government grant.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.060
And that whole process can
be pretty tedious for us.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.720
We have the problem
where grants roll over.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.120
A given grant might last
for a year or three,

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.410
and then it will stop.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.030
And in order to
continue doing work,

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:07.400
you have to have another
funding source to do it.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.340
So there's a lot
of moving projects

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.110
between different
billing accounts,

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.050
and tracking all of that
stuff is a big pain.

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.300
And I would say, on top of
all that, the biggest thing

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:21.190
is trying to control costs
to make sure that users don't

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:22.990
spend more than they intend.

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:25.720
For us, that's a bigger deal
than your average organization,

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.940
because if the grant
you have only provides,

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:33.610
let's say, $10,000 of
compute service resources

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.087
and you spend more than
that, the grant is not

00:03:36.087 --> 00:03:36.920
going to pay for it.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.363
So we just have to pay
for it out of other funds,

00:03:39.363 --> 00:03:40.780
and that's really
frustrating when

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:43.120
you can't do work you plan
to do because somebody

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.888
made a little mistake
in their cloud account.

00:03:45.888 --> 00:03:46.930
MATT LEONARD: Absolutely.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:48.860
Chris, would you like to take
it from a different perspective,

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:49.360
from Etsy?

00:03:51.238 --> 00:03:52.030
CHRIS HOOVER: Yeah.

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:53.440
Sorry, I had trouble
hearing the question.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:54.400
Could you repeat it, please?

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.192
MATT LEONARD: Sure.

00:03:55.192 --> 00:03:58.048
What's the hardest thing
about managing your GCP cost?

00:03:58.048 --> 00:03:58.840
CHRIS HOOVER: Yeah.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:02.140
For us, I think it's just
kind of a change in mindset.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:06.520
We come from a co-location
facility on bare metal.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:08.260
Resources and
consumption of them

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:10.780
work way differently there.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.750
The interaction between
us and our finance teams

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:15.140
is a little bit different.

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:16.640
I think there's a
lot to be learned,

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:20.500
in terms of how you
clarify assumptions

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:23.680
and levels of precision for
how we forecast together,

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.960
how we develop the tooling
together to make sure

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:29.230
that we're anticipating
costs with seasonality,

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.940
those sorts of things.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:34.610
So for us, it's kind of
learning new muscle memories

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:37.540
in our processes between
different organizations

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.650
to make them run more smoothly.

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:41.780
MATT LEONARD: Well,
that about covers it.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:43.270
So Shashank, from
your perspective

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:45.397
with managing
customers and partners

00:04:45.397 --> 00:04:47.230
and helping with the
migration to the cloud,

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:48.938
where do you see the
opportunity for them

00:04:48.938 --> 00:04:51.235
to add better controls
in their environment?

00:04:51.235 --> 00:04:52.360
SHASHANK JOSHI: Absolutely.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:54.400
I think the first
thing is no-brainer,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.380
that everybody should
enable their billing

00:04:56.380 --> 00:05:00.100
export in BigQuery, and you
should do that on day one.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:03.520
That definitely is one of the
learnings and the opportunity

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.320
that everybody
should be taking up.

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.150
Second thing is there
is enormous amount

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:09.200
of data and information there.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.910
So the more your team get
familiar with the data

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:15.010
and analyzes the data,
the more insights

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:18.280
you can draw from
your billing export.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:21.220
But I think one of the hardest
challenges that organizations

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.530
face is billing
is just one piece

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:25.490
of their entire IT organization.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:29.860
So how to overlay their
architecture onto GCP,

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:30.510
billing export.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.890
That's the important piece.

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:35.410
Because as Lukas
just mentioned, how

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.000
will you apportion or
attribute some of the costs

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.800
to different teams?

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:41.300
That's the hardest thing to do.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:44.000
And you can do it using,
for example, labels.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.800
And that gets you to define the
strategy beforehand, and not

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:50.060
just for today and not
just for near future,

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.390
but even for six months,
one year down the line.

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:55.780
Because it's really hard to
change the label strategy

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:59.300
once you decided
and going into that.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.270
So some of those lessons
and the opportunities,

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:03.100
in terms of deciding
that early, I

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:04.900
would conclude with one point.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:07.270
That one is not
every organization

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:10.660
recognizes the importance
of cost management early on.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.460
So there are so many
things that were

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:13.985
able, the foundation,
automation,

00:06:13.985 --> 00:06:14.860
this tool, that tool.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:17.565
But it's really
important to understand

00:06:17.565 --> 00:06:18.940
the importance of
cost management

00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:21.357
and think about it very early
in your cloud transformation

00:06:21.357 --> 00:06:22.557
journey.

00:06:22.557 --> 00:06:23.390
MATT LEONARD: Great.

00:06:23.390 --> 00:06:24.130
I mean, that's a
good point, though,

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:25.440
as you think about
your cost management

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:27.010
and how you organize your costs.

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:30.220
Dale, how's Vendasta approached
organizing their costs

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.653
within the cloud?

00:06:31.653 --> 00:06:33.820
DALE HOPKINS: So one of the
things that we have is--

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:35.830
I mean, we may take a little
bit different approach,

00:06:35.830 --> 00:06:37.288
but one of the
things we like to do

00:06:37.288 --> 00:06:40.540
is basically monitor
and alert on billing,

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:43.540
as opposed to actually setting
hard limits and things.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:46.660
For us, making sure that the
teams actually have a lot more

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:49.900
say and more understanding
of their actual cost

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:52.180
is a really important
part to getting developers

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:54.160
to actually understand
the cost implications

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:56.495
of the changes they're making.

00:06:56.495 --> 00:06:58.870
So I mean, really, for us,
it's a big part of the change,

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.220
is actually providing that data.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:02.530
And I think in a lot of
organizations, the teams

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:04.570
actually writing the
code don't understand

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:07.210
what it costs to operate
that code that they shipped.

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:10.090
And so for us, it's actually
a really useful input to them,

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:12.010
in terms of determining
when to refactor

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:13.780
and when to put more
effort into the code.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.298
I mean, cost is a really great
metric to help you with that.

00:07:16.298 --> 00:07:17.090
MATT LEONARD: Yeah.

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:18.298
It's a great point, actually.

00:07:18.298 --> 00:07:20.660
And so I'm going to stay
with you for a second.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:23.455
So what keeps you up at night
when you think about that?

00:07:23.455 --> 00:07:25.330
DALE HOPKINS: I guess,
for me, the stuff that

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:28.180
keeps you up at night on cloud
is the opposite stuff than when

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:29.580
you have an on-prem workload.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:31.150
Your on-prem
workload, I think, is

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:32.380
that you're concerned
that there's

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:34.360
going to be a huge surge
in load and you're not

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.340
going to be able to
serve your customers.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:38.007
The concern we have
is that you're going

00:07:38.007 --> 00:07:39.885
to have a huge surge in load.

00:07:39.885 --> 00:07:42.010
You're going to serve all
your customers just fine,

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:44.135
you're just not going to
have any money at the end.

00:07:44.135 --> 00:07:46.510
And so that's the thing,
especially with some of the--

00:07:46.510 --> 00:07:48.280
I mean, it's maybe the
double-edged sword of something

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:49.750
like an App Engine,
or a Pub/Sub,

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:52.960
or one of these services that
basically scales to infinity.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:56.790
That's awesome, but I mean, as
long as that's what you wanted.

00:07:56.790 --> 00:07:57.790
MATT LEONARD: Excellent.

00:07:57.790 --> 00:07:59.410
Well, I see Lukas
chuckling away here,

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:01.747
and I'm going to put this
one back on him as well.

00:08:01.747 --> 00:08:03.580
Because last year, Lukas
and I talked about,

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:05.500
how do you build better
financial governance controls

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:06.310
for your environment?

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:07.900
And Lukas talked
about it for an hour,

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:09.440
which was great,
because it meant

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:10.565
nobody had to listen to me.

00:08:10.565 --> 00:08:12.243
So Lukas?

00:08:12.243 --> 00:08:13.660
LUKAS KARLSSON:
So first, I'll say

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:17.980
that what keeps
us awake at night

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:20.740
is the idea that
someone might have

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:25.080
spent $100,000 in the cloud
while we were sleeping.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:30.490
And we had one incident on
another public cloud where

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:33.730
one of our users,
either intentionally

00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:37.360
or unintentionally, spent
$100,000 that they did not

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:38.530
have.

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:40.840
And as I alluded to,
that meant we just

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:43.870
had to pay the vendor from
another bucket of money that

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:45.850
was not intended
for that purpose.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:48.700
So that incident,
that one incident,

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.670
that happened several
years ago entirely

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:53.260
changed how everyone
at the Broad

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:55.590
thought about the
cloud and billing,

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:58.660
and created a lot of
fear and uncertainty

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:02.050
about what we might do,
eventually, in the cloud.

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:05.440
And so for us, it's been
really important to be

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:08.200
able to automate
all of these tasks.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:11.440
The idea of being able to
get notifications and email

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:15.700
from 300 billing accounts and
process all those notifications

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:19.450
and actually do something
appropriate to respond to them

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:20.480
is really challenging.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:23.630
So for us, automating
that has been key.

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:25.480
And last year,
Matt's team released

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:29.410
a feature, which I would say is
the second most important thing

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:32.500
you should enable in your
billing account after you get

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:37.480
started, which is to have
a budget, or many budgets,

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:41.412
that publish their
notifications to Pub/Sub.

00:09:41.412 --> 00:09:43.120
When you do that, you
can build something

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:46.870
like a Cloud Function that will
receive those notifications

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:49.795
and take potentially appropriate
action on them, which

00:09:49.795 --> 00:09:52.420
means that I don't have to read
every email that comes from all

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:55.150
these billing accounts and
decide what should be done.

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:57.560
I can write a Cloud
Function that can do that.

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:00.400
So with 300 or so
billing accounts,

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:05.080
we get like, 1,000 notifications
an hour from the billing

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:05.930
accounts.

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.290
And each time that
notification kicks off,

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:11.530
it kicks off a Cloud Function,
which updates a database, so

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:14.260
that we have a
single screen where

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:17.523
we can see the status of all
300 billing accounts at once,

00:10:17.523 --> 00:10:18.940
instead of having
to click through

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:22.390
to 300 billing account
consoles, which is not

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:24.760
something I would ever do.

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:26.590
And then in addition,
we added some logic,

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:31.420
where we could put some hard
thresholds in place that we

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:33.295
don't want to ever pass.

00:10:33.295 --> 00:10:36.400
And if one of these budgets
passes that threshold,

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:38.240
it can automatically
take an action.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:43.060
And so the example that we
showed in our talk last year

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:45.940
was three levels
of response, one

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:51.250
being notify someone, maybe
in Slack, or pager duty,

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:53.290
or something like that.

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:57.520
Another bigger hammer is to
identify all the VMs that

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:01.300
are currently running in that
billing account and stop them.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:05.530
This, for us, is a nice medium,
where we don't lose any data,

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:08.620
but we do, basically, stop
a majority of the spend.

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:11.260
So if we did have this
huge influx of spend,

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:15.190
it allows us to really shut
that down fairly quickly.

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:19.425
And then if you hit
another threshold that

00:11:19.425 --> 00:11:20.800
makes you very
uncomfortable, you

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.500
can do something like
just turn the billing off

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:26.140
on the projects and
they all stop working,

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:28.220
but you do not spend more money.

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:33.200
So for the engineering folks,
we don't really like that.

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:36.580
But for the folks in procurement
and in sponsored research,

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:39.490
who are managing the grants,
they absolutely love that.

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:42.520
They want to know for sure
that if you were allowed

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:45.490
to spend $10,000
and you hit $10,000,

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:48.590
that you have not spent a
single dollar more than that.

00:11:48.590 --> 00:11:50.650
And so implementing that
has certainly helped us

00:11:50.650 --> 00:11:51.627
manage that process.

00:11:51.627 --> 00:11:53.210
MATT LEONARD: So
that's a great segue.

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:55.085
So Shashank, you work
with a lot of customers

00:11:55.085 --> 00:11:59.770
and advise them on how to set up
and organize themselves on GCP.

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:02.785
What kind of best
practices do you recommend?

00:12:02.785 --> 00:12:03.660
SHASHANK JOSHI: Sure.

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:06.180
I touched up on that.

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:08.040
The first thing is
that understanding

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:10.890
the importance of cost
management solution in place,

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.900
and thinking about
it beforehand, not

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:14.080
as afterthought.

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:16.680
So while you're building
your platform or foundation,

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:18.270
at the same time,
or even before that,

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:20.395
you should be thinking
about your billing solution,

00:12:20.395 --> 00:12:22.140
billing analysis solution.

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:23.640
That's the first
thing I would say,

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:27.100
because not many
organizations think like that.

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:29.460
The second best practice
we say is, of course,

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:30.372
user billing export.

00:12:30.372 --> 00:12:31.830
The other features
are really good,

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:34.680
in terms of billing reports
and quartiles and alerts.

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:35.753
Those are really good.

00:12:35.753 --> 00:12:37.170
But if you need
more insights, you

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:39.320
need to use billing
export, BigQuery,

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:42.300
and analyze that yourself.

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:43.680
Of course, you
can start smaller.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:47.190
The other best practice we
say is it's like no-brainer,

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:51.270
in terms of don't do all the
required analysis beforehand.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:53.113
I want to build this
many dashboards.

00:12:53.113 --> 00:12:54.030
Don't start like that.

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:55.580
Start with initial dashboards.

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:58.170
Start in a [INAUDIBLE] fashion.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:01.300
When you start creating
dashboards and reports

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.720
and show it to stakeholders,
more and more stakeholders

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:06.075
will buy into your
initiative, and they'll

00:13:06.075 --> 00:13:08.200
give you more suggestions,
and you improve on that.

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:12.060
And that's how we have
operated with retail customers

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:12.580
with that.

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:15.960
For example, we started creating
new dashboard every two weeks.

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:20.790
And it can be a minor
improvement on the earlier

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:24.480
dashboard, but that really
helped get excitement

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:27.120
among the people, the
consumers of the dashboards.

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:30.840
And that really
led to that option

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:33.400
into that and the
importance of that.

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:35.430
And I would say
final best practice,

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:38.670
in terms of transparency
and visibility,

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:41.250
what we say is every cloud
transformation should

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:43.330
start with some kind
of a business plan.

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:45.740
And if you don't have that,
if you don't start with that,

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:47.700
now is the time to do it.

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:51.570
Because you need to have
what milestones, initiatives.

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:54.060
You need to break
down all of that

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.250
into the minor level details.

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:59.050
And then have a solution,
which can show you, hey,

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:02.500
your budget for this application
was this much for this month,

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:04.950
and where are you
vis-á-vis that?

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:08.700
Because what we have seen
is overall in one example.

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:12.720
So at a one quarter milestone,
overall, the organization,

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:15.870
was under the budget,
but they spent much more

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:18.360
on initiative 1
versus initiative 2.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:20.670
So even though overall
system looks good,

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:22.560
but you may be
overspending on something

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:24.240
and less spending
on something else.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:27.000
So it's very important
to go to granular level.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:29.310
To do a business case,
go to a granular level.

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:31.363
And I would say in
best case scenario

00:14:31.363 --> 00:14:33.030
is what we have seen
is they've gone all

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:34.800
the way to individual
applications

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:37.380
and individual environments
in that applications.

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:39.030
So you should know
what I'm supposed

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:42.400
to spend on this application on
dev environment in this month.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:44.420
So that granular level
is very important.

00:14:44.420 --> 00:14:45.760
MATT LEONARD: It's a
great point, actually.

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:48.302
So as you stop to think about
how you're organizing your cost

00:14:48.302 --> 00:14:50.660
and driving that budget
down to that granular level,

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:52.370
you start to introduce a
bit of a planning problem.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:53.910
Chris, I know at Etsy,
you guys have spent a bit

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:54.800
of time thinking about this.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:56.580
Do you want to talk a
little bit about how

00:14:56.580 --> 00:15:00.100
Etsy thinks about budgeting
and planning for the business?

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:01.500
CHRIS HOOVER: Sure, absolutely.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.200
It looks different for
different cadences,

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:06.660
different frequencies,
as you'd imagine,

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:09.390
with our finance cycles.

00:15:09.390 --> 00:15:12.990
So annually, we kick
off in about Q4.

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:15.990
So to think about all
of the roadmap items

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:17.640
for our architecture,
all of the things

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.530
that engineering wants
to get done, and look

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:22.080
at the consumption
that comes with those.

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:24.730
And then as we march
on throughout the year,

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:27.720
in the typical Agile
fashion, we innovate,

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:32.530
we adapt, and take a
few curves in the road.

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:36.060
And I think the key
there is transparency.

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:37.872
Early notification
of your finance teams

00:15:37.872 --> 00:15:39.330
will definitely
help them and build

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:41.910
confidence and trust
in your relationship

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:43.390
with them over time.

00:15:43.390 --> 00:15:48.138
It will also, even
implicitly, strengthen

00:15:48.138 --> 00:15:49.680
the mutual understanding
of the error

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:51.390
margins in those forecasts.

00:15:51.390 --> 00:15:53.090
As you go further
and further on,

00:15:53.090 --> 00:15:56.100
you get a feel for how each
other treats the numbers

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:58.560
and what level of
precision we're

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:04.110
able to get to at each point
in our maturity in the cloud.

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:07.770
For us, particularly
because we're mid-migration,

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:10.920
the types of things we're
working on evolves quickly

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:12.040
over time.

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:15.870
And so the type of system
or tool that we're using

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:19.133
and the team using
that uses those things

00:16:19.133 --> 00:16:20.800
is a very different
use case in problems

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:23.008
to solve, depending on where
it is in the engineering

00:16:23.008 --> 00:16:23.710
organization.

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:26.310
So how we manage
cost in search is

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:29.880
very different from how we
measure and manage costs

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:31.050
for a web platform.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:34.620
And so educating finance
on how these problems need

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:38.130
to be solved independently
and uniquely is also

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:39.627
a big part of the process.

00:16:39.627 --> 00:16:41.210
MATT LEONARD: So
that's a great point.

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:45.030
So Dale, Vendasta has got a
pretty mature offering now,

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:46.330
on top of Google Cloud.

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:48.180
And last year, you
talked a little bit

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:49.865
about how you drive
accountability and visibility

00:16:49.865 --> 00:16:50.430
in teams.

00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:51.450
Do you want to talk a
little bit about some

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:54.150
of what you've learned from
how you drive visibility

00:16:54.150 --> 00:16:55.442
down to your engineering teams?

00:16:55.442 --> 00:16:57.692
DALE HOPKINS: One of the
things we found really useful

00:16:57.692 --> 00:16:59.760
is the data export,
and that particularly

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:02.380
helps us because you don't
have to assign billing level

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:04.630
permissions to the teams to
have these sort of things,

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:06.797
but you can provide them
the data scope down to what

00:17:06.797 --> 00:17:07.829
they're interested in.

00:17:07.829 --> 00:17:10.800
And so for us, I mean, just
like so many other things

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:13.319
in your organization, as
soon as you make it visible,

00:17:13.319 --> 00:17:15.569
all of a sudden improvements
start to happen.

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:18.585
And so for us, just a big
part of that culture was like,

00:17:18.585 --> 00:17:19.710
hey guys, here's your data.

00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:22.349
And all of a sudden, that starts
to actually impact behavior.

00:17:22.349 --> 00:17:24.060
You don't even
have to tell people

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:25.890
what a good number
or a bad number is.

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:28.410
If you just show them a number
and explain how that relates

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:30.910
and that they affect that, that
made a really big difference

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:32.400
for us, in terms of cost.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:34.817
And then one of the other
things we found that was helpful

00:17:34.817 --> 00:17:38.250
was actually doing a
layer of this week's cost

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:39.930
versus last week's costs.

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:41.370
Because you're
going to find that

00:17:41.370 --> 00:17:43.650
it's pretty hard to
compare today to yesterday.

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:46.340
Because for us, specifically, we
got a lot of small businesses.

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:48.450
And if today's Saturday
and yesterday was Friday,

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:50.725
it's not a good comparison
because it just turns out

00:17:50.725 --> 00:17:52.350
that people tend to
do different things

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:53.517
on different days like that.

00:17:53.517 --> 00:17:55.110
And especially
weekly and monthly

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:57.690
overlays help the team, so
that not just presenting them,

00:17:57.690 --> 00:17:59.770
here's a number, but
here's a chart over time.

00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:02.250
And if it's up or down
a little bit, that's OK.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:04.832
But if it's way off, it
kind of gives teams the idea

00:18:04.832 --> 00:18:06.290
that they should
look at something.

00:18:06.290 --> 00:18:07.380
MATT LEONARD: Great.

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:10.210
This idea of seasonality, from
day-to-day or month to month,

00:18:10.210 --> 00:18:14.100
this is obviously something
that Etsy deals with a Q4 peak.

00:18:14.100 --> 00:18:17.430
How does Etsy think about
seasonal fluctuations?

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:20.910
CHRIS HOOVER: We've only got one
partial season under our belts,

00:18:20.910 --> 00:18:22.260
but I'll speak from that.

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:25.080
I think we've learned a lot
just in that short period.

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:27.900
We found it really
helpful to look back

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:30.090
at historical periods,
even in the data center,

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:36.900
and see how seasonality has
impacted our consumption.

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:40.860
And I would totally agree
that the micro aspect of that,

00:18:40.860 --> 00:18:43.910
the day-to-day, the
weekdays, is a big part,

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:47.400
and you want to look at how
that compounds with larger macro

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:51.330
seasonality, from annual
holidays to months of the year,

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:53.820
and everything in between.

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:57.000
Another great conversation to
have with your finance teams

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:01.050
is around what they
forecast for the next season

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:04.782
and melding that with your
consumption estimates,

00:19:04.782 --> 00:19:06.240
and therefore, your
cost estimates.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:08.400
That's a very healthy
and helpful conversation

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:11.280
to have with finance,
to marry the consumption

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:15.030
side and the forecasted
seasonality side,

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:17.730
and have that be
informed by what you've

00:19:17.730 --> 00:19:19.970
seen in previous
seasonality at all

00:19:19.970 --> 00:19:23.010
of those levels of granularity.

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:27.410
I'll also say that that also
varies by system or product.

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:31.260
So the economies of
scale will definitely

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:33.960
vary based on which GCP
products you're using

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:36.640
and which type of your
infrastructure you're using.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:41.460
So as an example, with visits
to our website, they may double,

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:45.480
but our consumption of compute
will far less than double,

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:48.060
which is good news, but we
want to be able to forecast

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:49.667
with that good news in advance.

00:19:49.667 --> 00:19:50.500
MATT LEONARD: Great.

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:53.270
I'm going to pass it over
to Lukas for a second.

00:19:53.270 --> 00:19:56.910
Lukas, you manage something
like 6,000 projects, 300 billing

00:19:56.910 --> 00:19:57.750
accounts.

00:19:57.750 --> 00:20:01.260
That's a fairly
distributed architecture,

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:04.440
and you've got, obviously,
some important stakeholders

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:07.350
in your planning process from
your accounting and finance

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:09.630
folks, down to your researchers.

00:20:09.630 --> 00:20:11.940
How do you manage across
a distributed group

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:13.890
like that in order to
get a plan so that you

00:20:13.890 --> 00:20:15.923
can build these budgets?

00:20:15.923 --> 00:20:17.340
LUKAS KARLSSON:
First, I just want

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:20.130
to correct the number
that you mentioned there.

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:23.520
I think we're at
25,000 projects now.

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:25.562
MATT LEONARD: My bad.

00:20:25.562 --> 00:20:27.270
LUKAS KARLSSON: Last
year, it was 10,000,

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:31.840
so there's been a lot
of growth since then.

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:35.700
So we have a pretty tight
onboarding process for cloud.

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:38.280
We use the same process for
all of the public clouds

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:40.620
that we support currently.

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:44.880
As I mentioned, most of our work
is funded by government grants.

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:47.940
And so internally,
each of those grants

00:20:47.940 --> 00:20:52.800
has a seven digit number that
we use as a cost identifier.

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:55.500
We call it a cost object.

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:58.830
And so that's what
people use to buy things,

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:02.190
whether their cloud
services, or supplies,

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:05.760
or whatever they need
for their science.

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:10.230
So users who have funding
and want to use the cloud

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:12.930
have to basically
submit a request

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:15.540
through a process
with procurement,

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:17.820
where they say, hey,
I have this funding

00:21:17.820 --> 00:21:19.780
and I want to use
it on the cloud.

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:22.770
And if we know,
based on the number,

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:26.070
that it's an administrative
funding source--

00:21:26.070 --> 00:21:28.440
like my IT budget
is an admin budget,

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:30.300
it's not funded by a grant--

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:32.850
then it mostly
just gets approved.

00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:34.650
The person who's
making the request

00:21:34.650 --> 00:21:37.170
is the authority on
that cost object,

00:21:37.170 --> 00:21:39.970
and they can decide
how much to spend.

00:21:39.970 --> 00:21:42.760
But if it's funded by
a government grant,

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:45.760
then the people who manage
the sponsor research funding

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:46.820
have to be involved.

00:21:46.820 --> 00:21:49.060
They have to check the
grant and make sure

00:21:49.060 --> 00:21:51.130
that it has enough
funding to support what

00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:52.560
the group is intending to do.

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:54.310
And then they also
have to check the grant

00:21:54.310 --> 00:21:56.560
and make sure it actually
allows you to spend money

00:21:56.560 --> 00:21:59.260
on the cloud.

00:21:59.260 --> 00:22:01.120
So all of that has to
happen before we even

00:22:01.120 --> 00:22:02.560
give them access.

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:04.670
And then once
everything is approved,

00:22:04.670 --> 00:22:07.150
we basically create
a new billing account

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:12.760
for that particular cost object
and give the user billing

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:13.990
account administrator.

00:22:13.990 --> 00:22:16.510
And now, they own
that billing account,

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:19.030
and they're responsible
for whatever happens in it.

00:22:19.030 --> 00:22:21.730
They can delegate
permissions to people

00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:25.150
within their team to create
projects, if they wish.

00:22:25.150 --> 00:22:27.262
Or if they don't have
that level of trust

00:22:27.262 --> 00:22:28.720
with the people on
their team, they

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:30.310
may choose to
create the projects

00:22:30.310 --> 00:22:33.370
themselves and give
the teammates access.

00:22:33.370 --> 00:22:37.150
Another common model
is users in our company

00:22:37.150 --> 00:22:38.650
are allowed to
create projects all

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:41.170
they want, they're just
not allowed to add billing,

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:43.630
unless they're a billing
account administrator.

00:22:43.630 --> 00:22:46.120
So people on your team
can create projects

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:48.070
and then they can give
you a sufficient level

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:51.640
of access to their project that
you can enable billing on it

00:22:51.640 --> 00:22:52.240
for them.

00:22:52.240 --> 00:22:54.880
And we have literally all
of these different models

00:22:54.880 --> 00:22:56.110
in place at the Broad.

00:22:56.110 --> 00:22:58.420
And in addition, we
have some situations

00:22:58.420 --> 00:23:03.370
where projects that we own
that are in our organization

00:23:03.370 --> 00:23:06.940
are being funded by someone
else's billing account.

00:23:06.940 --> 00:23:09.550
And we have situations
where projects

00:23:09.550 --> 00:23:11.710
that are owned by an
external organization

00:23:11.710 --> 00:23:14.000
are being funded by one
of our billing account.

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:17.140
So basically, every
scenario you could imagine,

00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:18.520
we have currently.

00:23:18.520 --> 00:23:20.740
But that process is what
we use, whether people

00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:22.780
are trying to get onboard
with Google or any

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:24.480
of the other public clouds.

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:25.480
MATT LEONARD: Excellent.

00:23:25.480 --> 00:23:28.090
All right, Shashank, you
advise a lot of customers.

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:30.400
How do you start estimating
costs for a new project,

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:32.020
like ballpark figures?

00:23:32.020 --> 00:23:33.670
What does that look like?

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:33.790
SHASHANK JOSHI: Sorry.

00:23:33.790 --> 00:23:34.458
Say that again.

00:23:34.458 --> 00:23:36.500
MATT LEONARD: So a customer
comes to you and say,

00:23:36.500 --> 00:23:38.500
hey, we'd like to move a
workload into Google Cloud

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:40.060
and we're planning to build
something completely new

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:40.700
from scratch.

00:23:40.700 --> 00:23:43.000
How do you even go about
ballparking that budget

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:44.320
estimate?

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:47.260
And what tools, I guess, are
available to you to do that?

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:49.052
SHASHANK JOSHI: That's
a really good point.

00:23:49.052 --> 00:23:52.300
And the best practice we
follow is during the migration

00:23:52.300 --> 00:23:56.230
analysis, we do the billing
view of that as well.

00:23:56.230 --> 00:24:00.180
So do the billing review very
early in your design cycle.

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:01.430
So that's a really good point.

00:24:01.430 --> 00:24:02.888
If you're not doing
that, it should

00:24:02.888 --> 00:24:04.130
be part of your governance.

00:24:04.130 --> 00:24:06.213
When you are planning for
a migration application,

00:24:06.213 --> 00:24:09.790
you should see how the
cost of that would be.

00:24:09.790 --> 00:24:12.910
Having said that, what we
have done to estimate the cost

00:24:12.910 --> 00:24:16.640
was looking at the trends,
looking at the usage,

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:18.070
at the customer.

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:20.590
We actually created
separate calculators,

00:24:20.590 --> 00:24:22.430
in terms of unit price.

00:24:22.430 --> 00:24:24.790
For example, if you're
using compute instances

00:24:24.790 --> 00:24:28.295
at a large scale, you would have
a certain amount of permitted

00:24:28.295 --> 00:24:30.170
use discounts, system
use discounts, and some

00:24:30.170 --> 00:24:32.310
of the other discounts,
and some percentage

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:33.800
of on demand pricing.

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:36.220
So your unit price
turns out to be

00:24:36.220 --> 00:24:39.670
for a compute, which is
vastly different from what

00:24:39.670 --> 00:24:41.230
you see on the website.

00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:43.510
So for all the services
that you're consuming,

00:24:43.510 --> 00:24:44.980
you should have
some kind of a unit

00:24:44.980 --> 00:24:48.320
price for your
organization worked out.

00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:51.220
That's not 100% accurate,
but that's close there.

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:55.180
And use those unit prices to
estimate your ballpark figure

00:24:55.180 --> 00:24:56.920
for your consumption.

00:24:56.920 --> 00:25:00.760
So if it's, let's say a
three-tier application,

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:03.207
you can say, how many
compute instances?

00:25:03.207 --> 00:25:03.790
How much data?

00:25:03.790 --> 00:25:05.050
How much egress?

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:07.330
So you know the unit
price for each service

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:11.360
and you can estimate the
price for that application.

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:13.450
But yeah, and I think
the interesting anecdote

00:25:13.450 --> 00:25:16.540
that me and Matt discussed
before was the unit price is

00:25:16.540 --> 00:25:18.490
not constant all the time.

00:25:18.490 --> 00:25:20.450
So coming back to the
seasonality question,

00:25:20.450 --> 00:25:22.570
so I was working
on the retailers.

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:26.280
And when you see this seasonal
Black Friday or Thanksgiving

00:25:26.280 --> 00:25:29.290
weekend, the spike
is much higher.

00:25:29.290 --> 00:25:32.160
And your committed
users, these commitments

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:33.980
are for a year or three years.

00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:36.170
You cannot make a
commitment for one month.

00:25:36.170 --> 00:25:37.860
So how do you manage that?

00:25:37.860 --> 00:25:41.190
And during that peak
cycle, on-demand use

00:25:41.190 --> 00:25:43.420
is going to go up significantly.

00:25:43.420 --> 00:25:46.990
So in your makeup of
compute instances,

00:25:46.990 --> 00:25:49.580
the ratio of on demand
is going to increase,

00:25:49.580 --> 00:25:51.920
and that comes at
a higher price.

00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:53.950
So your unit price,
during the peak,

00:25:53.950 --> 00:25:56.450
is higher than your
regular unit price.

00:25:56.450 --> 00:26:00.310
So when you estimate for,
let's say, a peak size,

00:26:00.310 --> 00:26:02.710
peak season, you're
not just having

00:26:02.710 --> 00:26:06.170
to estimate for the additional
capacity that you want,

00:26:06.170 --> 00:26:09.260
but that capacity is maybe
coming at a higher price.

00:26:09.260 --> 00:26:12.550
So using that seasonal
variation, also

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:15.650
the unit pricing, that's a
key to estimating the price.

00:26:15.650 --> 00:26:16.650
MATT LEONARD: Excellent.

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:18.590
So there's been a number of
questions from the audience.

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:20.173
I'm just going to
try and collate them

00:26:20.173 --> 00:26:21.800
together in a single one.

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:23.500
What tools are
you using in order

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:28.030
to organize, and then view,
and then internally chargeback

00:26:28.030 --> 00:26:28.750
those costs?

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:32.890
I'm going to ask you, Chris,
as a big corporation that

00:26:32.890 --> 00:26:36.400
obviously has many departments
running infrastructure on GCP.

00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:39.008
What are you using
to manage this?

00:26:39.008 --> 00:26:39.800
CHRIS HOOVER: Sure.

00:26:39.800 --> 00:26:42.860
Right now, we're using
the Native Cloud Console.

00:26:42.860 --> 00:26:46.165
It's grown a good bit
in the last year or so.

00:26:46.165 --> 00:26:48.040
We're seeing some new
functionality come out.

00:26:48.040 --> 00:26:50.340
I know the Billing Product
management team at Google

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:52.730
is hard at work and
rolling out new features,

00:26:52.730 --> 00:26:55.700
getting visibility into
different types of discounts

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:57.270
within the bill.

00:26:57.270 --> 00:27:00.300
So we are anxiously awaiting
more development there.

00:27:00.300 --> 00:27:03.500
That's kind of a factor
in how we're developing

00:27:03.500 --> 00:27:04.640
our own use of tools.

00:27:04.640 --> 00:27:07.520
So let me run through the
list of the rest of the things

00:27:07.520 --> 00:27:09.800
that we're currently
using, BigQuery, Data

00:27:09.800 --> 00:27:13.400
Studio, and Looker, as well,
for our current dashboards.

00:27:13.400 --> 00:27:17.060
Also, in the Agile fashion of
rolling those out incrementally

00:27:17.060 --> 00:27:19.670
to kind of suit the
need and show people

00:27:19.670 --> 00:27:23.810
and let that data
drive action naturally.

00:27:23.810 --> 00:27:25.700
We have kind of a
two-pronged need

00:27:25.700 --> 00:27:28.430
here, not just for billing,
but also for our sustainability

00:27:28.430 --> 00:27:28.970
goals.

00:27:28.970 --> 00:27:31.800
We're very interested not
just in cost efficiency,

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:34.610
but also in the efficiencies
of the resources

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:36.850
we use, the energy of the
pull from the environment

00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:37.970
and measuring that.

00:27:37.970 --> 00:27:40.220
So we have double
motivation at Etsy

00:27:40.220 --> 00:27:42.040
to really get the tooling right.

00:27:42.040 --> 00:27:46.790
And in this early stage
of our tooling roadmap,

00:27:46.790 --> 00:27:49.130
we're kind of
merging and syncing

00:27:49.130 --> 00:27:53.090
the paces of that internal
tool development, the use

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:56.630
of the already provided tools,
and understanding where Google

00:27:56.630 --> 00:27:58.760
is developing its
own functionality

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:02.000
for future features to get
an idea of what this looks

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:04.030
like in a steady state for us.

00:28:04.030 --> 00:28:06.775
I think there's still a
good bit more ahead for us.

00:28:06.775 --> 00:28:08.900
MATT LEONARD: And I'll pass
it on to Dale, as well.

00:28:08.900 --> 00:28:11.732
Dale, you've built some pretty
cool stuff using DataShare.

00:28:11.732 --> 00:28:13.190
How do you share
those costs around

00:28:13.190 --> 00:28:14.725
within your organization?

00:28:14.725 --> 00:28:16.850
DALE HOPKINS: How many of
you in the audience here,

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:19.340
with a show of hands,
use Data Studio currently

00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:22.100
in your organization? 'Cause
it's not nearly as many

00:28:22.100 --> 00:28:23.315
as I might have thought.

00:28:23.315 --> 00:28:25.190
Data Studio is
actually really great.

00:28:25.190 --> 00:28:26.910
It allows you to build
these dashboards.

00:28:26.910 --> 00:28:28.368
And so one of the
things we've done

00:28:28.368 --> 00:28:30.650
is we've actually put together
Data Studio dashboards

00:28:30.650 --> 00:28:33.470
and they share, kind of like
Google Docs, not quite as good,

00:28:33.470 --> 00:28:35.065
but sort of similar idea.

00:28:35.065 --> 00:28:36.440
And you can actually
put these up

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:38.880
on monitors just in the hall.

00:28:38.880 --> 00:28:40.850
And so you can actually,
in a team's room,

00:28:40.850 --> 00:28:42.392
have their costs right there.

00:28:42.392 --> 00:28:43.850
And so they're
literally confronted

00:28:43.850 --> 00:28:47.420
by a chart of their costs
when they walk in the door.

00:28:47.420 --> 00:28:49.085
So I mean, it's a
simple tool and it

00:28:49.085 --> 00:28:50.960
queries pretty much
anything out of BigQuery,

00:28:50.960 --> 00:28:53.735
or any other Google source,
and it's really quite easy.

00:28:53.735 --> 00:28:56.360
So I'd encourage you to give it
a shot, because it's really not

00:28:56.360 --> 00:28:56.990
as intimidating.

00:28:56.990 --> 00:28:58.610
And it makes graphs
that are beautiful,

00:28:58.610 --> 00:29:00.027
and I don't make
beautiful graphs,

00:29:00.027 --> 00:29:01.832
so that's kind of exciting.

00:29:01.832 --> 00:29:03.290
I mean, the other
tool that I think

00:29:03.290 --> 00:29:04.748
is everybody's
favorite that nobody

00:29:04.748 --> 00:29:07.358
likes to admit they use is
Sheets, which is awesome, too.

00:29:07.358 --> 00:29:09.650
Because you can just dump
raw data in there, and that's

00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:11.692
something to play with
and figure out beforehand.

00:29:11.692 --> 00:29:13.790
But those are the two
tools that we use.

00:29:13.790 --> 00:29:15.880
One of them is much
prettier than the other.

00:29:15.880 --> 00:29:16.790
MATT LEONARD: For
any of you that

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:18.665
might be feeling
intimidated about DataShare,

00:29:18.665 --> 00:29:19.818
I'm just a product manager.

00:29:19.818 --> 00:29:21.860
So I was like, I don't
know about this DataShare,

00:29:21.860 --> 00:29:25.237
till after watching Dale
build these amazing dashboards

00:29:25.237 --> 00:29:27.320
in about seven minutes on
stage at Next last year,

00:29:27.320 --> 00:29:28.410
I was like, I'm an expert.

00:29:28.410 --> 00:29:30.117
This thing is super easy.

00:29:30.117 --> 00:29:32.450
So definitely encourage any
of you to take a look at it.

00:29:32.450 --> 00:29:35.120
We're going to take another one.

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:37.820
How much time do
you spend, Lukas,

00:29:37.820 --> 00:29:40.533
on managing billing data across
your multi-cloud deployments?

00:29:40.533 --> 00:29:41.950
I'm not going to
pick on GCP here.

00:29:41.950 --> 00:29:44.410
I'm going to make
it multi-cloud.

00:29:44.410 --> 00:29:48.050
LUKAS KARLSSON: Way more
than I ever expected.

00:29:48.050 --> 00:29:49.490
I'm a tech guy.

00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:54.110
I like to build big scaling
systems in the cloud.

00:29:54.110 --> 00:29:58.850
And it turns out that I'm one
of the world's experts on Google

00:29:58.850 --> 00:30:02.510
billing now, which is not
ever something I expected

00:30:02.510 --> 00:30:04.410
to have happen.

00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:07.430
So it's quite a bit of time.

00:30:07.430 --> 00:30:08.930
I think you guys
have certainly made

00:30:08.930 --> 00:30:12.470
it easier over the last year or
two with all the new features.

00:30:12.470 --> 00:30:14.970
We've been a GCP
customer for a long time.

00:30:14.970 --> 00:30:18.560
So before the BigQuery
feature existed,

00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:22.280
we were manually importing our
CSVs every day into BigQuery

00:30:22.280 --> 00:30:24.785
so that we had some
visibility into the data.

00:30:24.785 --> 00:30:26.660
And then when you guys
released that feature,

00:30:26.660 --> 00:30:29.430
we didn't have to build
that ETL process anymore.

00:30:29.430 --> 00:30:32.120
We just turn on the switch.

00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:35.420
Before we had access
to Data Studio,

00:30:35.420 --> 00:30:37.340
we were already a Tableau shop.

00:30:37.340 --> 00:30:39.290
So we put a lot of
effort into building

00:30:39.290 --> 00:30:42.470
some really advanced Tableau
reports that do some very

00:30:42.470 --> 00:30:43.700
broad specific things.

00:30:43.700 --> 00:30:47.420
Like when you have grant
funding and then you

00:30:47.420 --> 00:30:49.880
use that grant funding to
pay for a cloud service,

00:30:49.880 --> 00:30:52.370
there's an overhead tax
that gets applied to that

00:30:52.370 --> 00:30:54.480
as part of your organization.

00:30:54.480 --> 00:30:56.390
And so we need to
let our users see

00:30:56.390 --> 00:30:58.880
what the full loaded cost
of the cloud is going to be,

00:30:58.880 --> 00:31:01.190
not what the actual bill
from Google is going to be,

00:31:01.190 --> 00:31:02.690
because it's not the same.

00:31:02.690 --> 00:31:05.690
So we have all of those
tools built into Tableau.

00:31:05.690 --> 00:31:07.850
And then when Data
Studio came out,

00:31:07.850 --> 00:31:12.050
we have built a bunch of
visualizations in Data Studio.

00:31:12.050 --> 00:31:14.990
Many of those have actually
been replaced by the features

00:31:14.990 --> 00:31:17.480
that you guys have
built into the console.

00:31:17.480 --> 00:31:21.260
So I think now, the thing
I use the most, by far, is

00:31:21.260 --> 00:31:24.260
the reports in the
console, absolutely.

00:31:24.260 --> 00:31:26.390
And these other tools,
sometimes we actually

00:31:26.390 --> 00:31:28.970
have to take new features
that you guys have brought,

00:31:28.970 --> 00:31:32.840
like the recent release
of the invoice month.

00:31:32.840 --> 00:31:36.080
And we're now introducing those
features into the Data Studio,

00:31:36.080 --> 00:31:38.690
into the Tableau tools
that we use as well.

00:31:38.690 --> 00:31:42.510
But between me and
Stuart in procurement,

00:31:42.510 --> 00:31:45.920
we spend many, many hours
a month dealing with this.

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:49.940
I mean, just the time that
he spends taking the bill

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:53.990
and charging back to 300
different internal cost

00:31:53.990 --> 00:31:57.080
objects, 30 or 40
of which will have

00:31:57.080 --> 00:31:59.250
expired since the last month.

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:01.310
So for each of those,
he has to go figure

00:32:01.310 --> 00:32:03.080
out what the new
funding source is,

00:32:03.080 --> 00:32:05.150
and it's such a huge burden.

00:32:05.150 --> 00:32:06.830
So we're constantly
working on ways

00:32:06.830 --> 00:32:09.830
to automate that,
building App Engine apps

00:32:09.830 --> 00:32:12.980
that he uses to
manage the accounts,

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:16.017
and giving him more reports
in Data Studio, and so forth.

00:32:16.017 --> 00:32:16.850
MATT LEONARD: Great.

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:18.760
I mean, Chris, you have a
similar type of environment.

00:32:18.760 --> 00:32:19.310
Sorry, Shashank.

00:32:19.310 --> 00:32:20.750
I'm going to come back
to you in a minute.

00:32:20.750 --> 00:32:22.375
You have a similar
type of environment.

00:32:22.375 --> 00:32:23.870
How much time is
your organization

00:32:23.870 --> 00:32:27.350
spending and dedicated resources
to organizing and charging

00:32:27.350 --> 00:32:29.120
back costs?

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:30.870
CHRIS HOOVER: We have
one billing account,

00:32:30.870 --> 00:32:33.930
which makes it a little
bit simpler in comparison.

00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:36.260
But it is,
nevertheless, complex.

00:32:36.260 --> 00:32:41.570
We've been very thankful
in standing by the decision

00:32:41.570 --> 00:32:44.700
to hire a dedicated resource
to manage this full-time.

00:32:44.700 --> 00:32:46.050
So we have a program manager.

00:32:46.050 --> 00:32:48.740
You might find an analyst
to be helpful in spending

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:51.680
full-time focus
and understanding

00:32:51.680 --> 00:32:55.130
and forecasting, sort
of as a liaison to all

00:32:55.130 --> 00:32:58.880
of your engineering teams, by
products that they're using

00:32:58.880 --> 00:33:02.900
and by roadmap type, what
they're doing in Google Cloud.

00:33:02.900 --> 00:33:06.470
I think you'll quickly find
that that type of person's time

00:33:06.470 --> 00:33:11.480
is consumed on very
meaningful things.

00:33:11.480 --> 00:33:13.125
At Etsy, that person--

00:33:13.125 --> 00:33:15.170
Danny, he might be here.

00:33:15.170 --> 00:33:17.390
Wave to Danny.

00:33:17.390 --> 00:33:22.610
He floats between finance and
accounting and engineering,

00:33:22.610 --> 00:33:24.510
and speaks very
knowledgeably about all

00:33:24.510 --> 00:33:27.080
of these things,
along with Google,

00:33:27.080 --> 00:33:30.470
and is kind of a single
library of information

00:33:30.470 --> 00:33:33.300
that really helps us
connect these things.

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:35.130
So if you haven't
already, or if you're

00:33:35.130 --> 00:33:38.480
in the early stages of
starting on the cloud,

00:33:38.480 --> 00:33:40.640
I think, as part of
resource planning,

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:42.530
that people planning
and an analyst

00:33:42.530 --> 00:33:45.620
or a program manager to do
these things proactively

00:33:45.620 --> 00:33:47.255
is a really smart call.

00:33:47.255 --> 00:33:48.130
MATT LEONARD: Thanks.

00:33:48.130 --> 00:33:49.880
And we see that quite
a bit, that customers

00:33:49.880 --> 00:33:51.672
have somebody who's
dedicated and committed

00:33:51.672 --> 00:33:53.990
to what does that cost
management story look

00:33:53.990 --> 00:33:55.400
like in the cloud.

00:33:55.400 --> 00:33:58.550
I mean, so Shashank, we
got a lot of questions

00:33:58.550 --> 00:34:01.940
from the audience today about,
how do we organize the cost?

00:34:01.940 --> 00:34:03.740
Obviously, Google has folders.

00:34:03.740 --> 00:34:04.610
They have projects.

00:34:04.610 --> 00:34:05.970
These are all visible
in the billing data.

00:34:05.970 --> 00:34:08.060
But one of things we
haven't really talked about

00:34:08.060 --> 00:34:09.770
is labels and label strategy.

00:34:09.770 --> 00:34:13.190
How do customers approach
a labeling strategy

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:14.215
for their environment?

00:34:14.215 --> 00:34:15.340
SHASHANK JOSHI: Absolutely.

00:34:15.340 --> 00:34:17.690
I think this is, I think,
the most important part

00:34:17.690 --> 00:34:20.510
for the larger
organizations, because GCP

00:34:20.510 --> 00:34:23.000
exists into a
larger IT ecosystem

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:24.110
for big organizations.

00:34:24.110 --> 00:34:26.179
So there are lines
of businesses that

00:34:26.179 --> 00:34:29.510
have vice presidents, department
heads, and application owners.

00:34:29.510 --> 00:34:32.929
So there is a big organization
structure outside of GCP.

00:34:32.929 --> 00:34:35.330
So how will you impose that?

00:34:35.330 --> 00:34:37.699
Because nobody wants to
see overall GCP cost.

00:34:37.699 --> 00:34:40.610
They would want to see,
what is my team's cost?

00:34:40.610 --> 00:34:42.080
Or if it is a vice
president, they

00:34:42.080 --> 00:34:44.510
would want to see, what
are all the applications

00:34:44.510 --> 00:34:47.659
under his line of business?

00:34:47.659 --> 00:34:51.060
And how they are doing vis-á-vis
the budget that they have.

00:34:51.060 --> 00:34:53.491
So it's very important
how do you do that.

00:34:53.491 --> 00:34:55.699
And there are two ways to
do that, as Matt mentioned.

00:34:55.699 --> 00:34:58.750
One way, recently announced, I
think comparatively recently,

00:34:58.750 --> 00:35:04.340
is you can align your organized
structure with the GCP resource

00:35:04.340 --> 00:35:05.150
organization.

00:35:05.150 --> 00:35:07.430
And by that, I mean
your folder structure.

00:35:07.430 --> 00:35:09.920
So you can have a folder
structure dedicated

00:35:09.920 --> 00:35:12.860
to vice president, and
everything related to him

00:35:12.860 --> 00:35:14.120
is under that.

00:35:14.120 --> 00:35:17.375
And that data flows
into your billing export

00:35:17.375 --> 00:35:18.780
with Project Ancestry.

00:35:18.780 --> 00:35:22.130
So you can use that field
to identify this project

00:35:22.130 --> 00:35:23.680
belongs to this VP.

00:35:23.680 --> 00:35:26.460
So this is very
simple to start with.

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:27.890
But you may get into challenges.

00:35:27.890 --> 00:35:30.105
Because if there are
projects shared by two VPs,

00:35:30.105 --> 00:35:32.480
or there is a project that is
not assigned to any one VP,

00:35:32.480 --> 00:35:35.270
or projects moves from
one folder to one other,

00:35:35.270 --> 00:35:38.710
there are complications
related to that.

00:35:38.710 --> 00:35:40.640
And of course, this is
at the project level.

00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:43.190
So what if you want to
go beyond the project?

00:35:43.190 --> 00:35:45.410
If you're running, let's
say, different applications

00:35:45.410 --> 00:35:46.785
within one project,
how would you

00:35:46.785 --> 00:35:48.960
distribute that, or
different environments?

00:35:48.960 --> 00:35:52.340
So the best way of doing
that is using labels.

00:35:52.340 --> 00:35:56.740
And label strategy, as I said,
you cannot change it often.

00:35:56.740 --> 00:36:00.310
You are decided very early
on, and not just for today.

00:36:00.310 --> 00:36:03.380
As I said before, you are to
do it for three months or six

00:36:03.380 --> 00:36:04.640
months down the line as well.

00:36:04.640 --> 00:36:06.490
Because data hardware
change labels

00:36:06.490 --> 00:36:09.480
know when you are
already in process.

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:12.260
And I would say some examples
of that using, for example,

00:36:12.260 --> 00:36:15.320
it can be a vice
president or department.

00:36:15.320 --> 00:36:16.850
It can be by application.

00:36:16.850 --> 00:36:19.340
It can be by environment.

00:36:19.340 --> 00:36:22.790
And I've seen uses, where
even the operations team uses

00:36:22.790 --> 00:36:24.710
the labels, for example,
for batching cycles,

00:36:24.710 --> 00:36:25.380
and all of that.

00:36:25.380 --> 00:36:26.870
So it's not only
billing related,

00:36:26.870 --> 00:36:29.210
but it's really significant,
in terms of billing.

00:36:29.210 --> 00:36:33.520
And all of these label data
flows into your GCP export,

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:35.790
billing export, in BigQuery.

00:36:35.790 --> 00:36:37.378
And again, there are two labels.

00:36:37.378 --> 00:36:38.420
There are project labels.

00:36:38.420 --> 00:36:39.503
There are resource labels.

00:36:39.503 --> 00:36:41.470
So you can even
drill down further.

00:36:41.470 --> 00:36:44.620
The opportunities are immense,
but it's very complex as well.

00:36:44.620 --> 00:36:46.450
Because initially, we struggled.

00:36:46.450 --> 00:36:49.090
I remember two years
ago when labels came,

00:36:49.090 --> 00:36:52.300
and we tried to use it
in the very beginning.

00:36:52.300 --> 00:36:53.960
It's a nested feel in BigQuery.

00:36:53.960 --> 00:36:57.250
And when we applied the sum of
everything and all of our costs

00:36:57.250 --> 00:37:00.145
went up five times, and
everybody was shocked.

00:37:00.145 --> 00:37:02.710
Then we realized.

00:37:02.710 --> 00:37:04.870
I would say, it's
really important

00:37:04.870 --> 00:37:07.750
that your team is familiar
with the export schema.

00:37:07.750 --> 00:37:10.600
And the number of
services that are launched

00:37:10.600 --> 00:37:12.790
so frequently by Google
Cloud, the schema

00:37:12.790 --> 00:37:14.210
is changing all the time.

00:37:14.210 --> 00:37:17.080
For example, this folder budget
ancestry feature was not there

00:37:17.080 --> 00:37:18.550
last year, I believe.

00:37:18.550 --> 00:37:23.140
So a lot of new features
are coming into export,

00:37:23.140 --> 00:37:26.380
so it's very important that
your team is familiar and adapt

00:37:26.380 --> 00:37:28.735
to really change the schema.

00:37:28.735 --> 00:37:29.860
MATT LEONARD: Yeah, thanks.

00:37:29.860 --> 00:37:31.860
There's lots of props
that, for all the features

00:37:31.860 --> 00:37:34.300
that we're launching, we are
trying to improve things.

00:37:34.300 --> 00:37:36.310
For those of you who
didn't catch it earlier,

00:37:36.310 --> 00:37:38.080
Target did a presentation
earlier today

00:37:38.080 --> 00:37:40.288
where they talked about
their label strategy and some

00:37:40.288 --> 00:37:42.760
of the mistakes that
they made, not ensuring

00:37:42.760 --> 00:37:45.067
that they had consistent
label strategy

00:37:45.067 --> 00:37:46.900
across their organization
early on, and then

00:37:46.900 --> 00:37:47.830
in terms of governance.

00:37:47.830 --> 00:37:50.247
So if you didn't see that, it
will be available on YouTube

00:37:50.247 --> 00:37:50.920
after Next.

00:37:50.920 --> 00:37:53.128
But they talk about how they
use [INAUDIBLE] in order

00:37:53.128 --> 00:37:55.030
to govern their
environment and make sure

00:37:55.030 --> 00:37:57.438
that their resources
are labeled consistently

00:37:57.438 --> 00:37:58.480
across their environment.

00:37:58.480 --> 00:37:59.510
So it's a great session.

00:37:59.510 --> 00:37:59.900
SHASHANK JOSHI: Yeah.

00:37:59.900 --> 00:38:01.525
I think one point,
as well on this one,

00:38:01.525 --> 00:38:03.470
where we decided
to label strategy,

00:38:03.470 --> 00:38:06.010
we also had a labeling
compliance dashboard.

00:38:06.010 --> 00:38:09.370
So we built a dashboard on
how compliant every project

00:38:09.370 --> 00:38:10.340
is on labels.

00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:12.060
So if you're missing
a label, we'll

00:38:12.060 --> 00:38:14.368
show that as like you
missed the compliance.

00:38:14.368 --> 00:38:15.160
MATT LEONARD: Yeah.

00:38:15.160 --> 00:38:16.930
And so what we're working on
some tools to help with that,

00:38:16.930 --> 00:38:18.880
but definitely check
out the Target session.

00:38:18.880 --> 00:38:19.730
It's very useful.

00:38:19.730 --> 00:38:22.300
Now, the top upvoted
question here from the Dory

00:38:22.300 --> 00:38:26.400
is actually, how do I
reduce my GCP costs?

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:29.080
Who'd like to take
that one first?

00:38:29.080 --> 00:38:30.837
Excellent.

00:38:30.837 --> 00:38:32.670
DALE HOPKINS: There's
lots of different ways

00:38:32.670 --> 00:38:34.020
to reduce your costs.

00:38:34.020 --> 00:38:36.480
I guess this kind of depends
on your particular story

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:38.330
and your application.

00:38:38.330 --> 00:38:40.080
So some of the ways
that we've done things

00:38:40.080 --> 00:38:42.820
is we started on
GCP a long time ago,

00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:46.830
and some of the cost model,
we're on Python 2-7 App Engine.

00:38:46.830 --> 00:38:48.900
Migrating to a more
modern stack can often

00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:51.780
change the efficiency,
usage as memor, and CPU,

00:38:51.780 --> 00:38:55.260
and also the cost model on
some of the GKE resources

00:38:55.260 --> 00:38:57.420
being lower than those
of App Engine standard.

00:38:57.420 --> 00:38:59.690
That's one way that, for
us, that's our primary cost

00:38:59.690 --> 00:39:01.440
reduction model, is
actually moving things

00:39:01.440 --> 00:39:04.470
from App Engine standard
into a more modern runtime.

00:39:04.470 --> 00:39:06.930
I mean, App Engine standard
has modern runtimes, but not

00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:08.090
the one that we use.

00:39:08.090 --> 00:39:10.590
One of the other ways is,
obviously, sitting down,

00:39:10.590 --> 00:39:12.210
look at your GCP bill.

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:15.540
And if you are a continuous
user, and at the end of it,

00:39:15.540 --> 00:39:17.582
you see a sustained
use discount,

00:39:17.582 --> 00:39:19.290
I mean, that's an
opportunity right there

00:39:19.290 --> 00:39:21.060
to do a committed discount,
if that's something

00:39:21.060 --> 00:39:22.140
that you actually think.

00:39:22.140 --> 00:39:24.072
So I mean, obviously,
don't do that if you

00:39:24.072 --> 00:39:25.530
have a ton of
seasonal fluctuation,

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:29.210
but that's something that
we've also done to save cost.

00:39:29.210 --> 00:39:30.735
Those are the big ones for us.

00:39:30.735 --> 00:39:32.610
MATT LEONARD: I'm going
to let everybody take

00:39:32.610 --> 00:39:34.290
this one, Chris, take it away.

00:39:34.290 --> 00:39:35.748
CHRIS HOOVER:
Preemptibles might be

00:39:35.748 --> 00:39:38.040
another one along
very similar lines,

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:39.330
depending on the workloads.

00:39:39.330 --> 00:39:41.170
CUDs would be on one
end of the spectrum,

00:39:41.170 --> 00:39:42.628
preemptibles would
be on the other,

00:39:42.628 --> 00:39:45.720
but both are really high
cost savings off the list.

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:47.640
Really depends on what
products you're using.

00:39:47.640 --> 00:39:49.590
I mean, if you're
heavy storage users,

00:39:49.590 --> 00:39:52.080
you should weigh Nearline
versus Coldline and all

00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:55.650
the other storage options
with what you actually need.

00:39:55.650 --> 00:39:57.540
I think autoscaling
is also a great thing

00:39:57.540 --> 00:40:01.230
to look into, across the board.

00:40:01.230 --> 00:40:03.290
Some things are a
little bit more complex,

00:40:03.290 --> 00:40:07.020
and you might want to form a
short-term squad, so to speak,

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:09.780
of cross-functional team members
from different engineering

00:40:09.780 --> 00:40:13.620
teams to look into what
could non-destructively

00:40:13.620 --> 00:40:15.700
create some efficiencies.

00:40:15.700 --> 00:40:17.730
There's a little
bit of research that

00:40:17.730 --> 00:40:19.110
could go a long
way, potentially,

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:20.652
in finding things
that you could just

00:40:20.652 --> 00:40:22.590
use in a slightly
different way but still get

00:40:22.590 --> 00:40:26.145
the same benefits
from that consumption.

00:40:26.145 --> 00:40:27.020
SHASHANK JOSHI: Sure.

00:40:27.020 --> 00:40:29.660
And we recommend to approach
this in multiple ways.

00:40:29.660 --> 00:40:32.530
One part of
governance is biweekly

00:40:32.530 --> 00:40:35.740
or weekly, we have
cost optimization

00:40:35.740 --> 00:40:37.250
reports, dashboards.

00:40:37.250 --> 00:40:39.340
And the way we look
at that is everybody

00:40:39.340 --> 00:40:41.330
looks at the top
five cost drivers.

00:40:41.330 --> 00:40:45.220
So if Compute Engine was higher
for you than your estimation,

00:40:45.220 --> 00:40:46.047
you drill down.

00:40:46.047 --> 00:40:47.630
So that's where the
dashboard come in.

00:40:47.630 --> 00:40:49.870
So if compute was high,
why it was higher?

00:40:49.870 --> 00:40:53.920
So in many cases, we realized
licensing cost was higher.

00:40:53.920 --> 00:40:55.870
So is there a way you
can optimize that,

00:40:55.870 --> 00:40:59.170
using your own licensing.

00:40:59.170 --> 00:41:03.240
And sometimes your storage
costs were higher for disks.

00:41:03.240 --> 00:41:06.020
That's where another
good feature comes in.

00:41:06.020 --> 00:41:08.560
You have to marry your billing
data with the platform data.

00:41:08.560 --> 00:41:13.420
So in one case, we had a lot
of custom GCP Python scripts

00:41:13.420 --> 00:41:15.970
just going around and finding
how many orphan disks that you

00:41:15.970 --> 00:41:18.690
have, the disks which are
not connected to any server

00:41:18.690 --> 00:41:20.770
and nobody knows
that they exist.

00:41:20.770 --> 00:41:23.470
So that cost gets
added to your thing.

00:41:23.470 --> 00:41:28.000
So you're to marry your billing
export with the platform data,

00:41:28.000 --> 00:41:30.620
so you get platform data
[INAUDIBLE] into that.

00:41:30.620 --> 00:41:34.630
And finally, I would say you
have to make the billing review

00:41:34.630 --> 00:41:36.460
as part of your design process.

00:41:36.460 --> 00:41:38.770
So you are to push the billing
related thing very early

00:41:38.770 --> 00:41:41.290
into your design cycle.

00:41:41.290 --> 00:41:44.090
I would say 99% of the
times, it's afterthought.

00:41:44.090 --> 00:41:46.838
They launch the application,
migrate the application.

00:41:46.838 --> 00:41:48.380
They do their cheers
and all of that,

00:41:48.380 --> 00:41:51.520
but then a month later, they
see the cost has gone higher.

00:41:51.520 --> 00:41:55.530
So best practice is to really
do the review much earlier on.

00:41:55.530 --> 00:41:57.610
Do the optimization
using autoscaling,

00:41:57.610 --> 00:41:59.278
or any other features
of optimization

00:41:59.278 --> 00:42:01.570
early on before you launch
an application on the cloud.

00:42:04.590 --> 00:42:06.140
LUKAS KARLSSON: So let's see.

00:42:06.140 --> 00:42:09.830
I would say Dale hit on
probably the best thing to do

00:42:09.830 --> 00:42:12.770
is just really make it all
visible to the people who

00:42:12.770 --> 00:42:14.070
are dealing with it.

00:42:14.070 --> 00:42:17.675
So if they users who are doing
cloud don't look at their bill

00:42:17.675 --> 00:42:19.550
and don't know what's
getting spent in there,

00:42:19.550 --> 00:42:20.925
the likelihood
that they're going

00:42:20.925 --> 00:42:25.070
to change their behavior to
reduce cost is absolutely no.

00:42:25.070 --> 00:42:28.010
So making it as visible as
possible and as easy for them

00:42:28.010 --> 00:42:33.440
to understand how their behavior
is affecting the costs is huge.

00:42:33.440 --> 00:42:37.400
Also, I would also
reiterate that educating

00:42:37.400 --> 00:42:40.670
people on the different
services and how they're charged

00:42:40.670 --> 00:42:41.670
is really key.

00:42:41.670 --> 00:42:44.990
If someone deployed an App
Engine app on App Engine

00:42:44.990 --> 00:42:47.570
Flexible because that was
their preference at the time,

00:42:47.570 --> 00:42:50.600
and they don't realize
that app runs 24/7,

00:42:50.600 --> 00:42:52.700
whereas an App Engine
in standard app

00:42:52.700 --> 00:42:55.610
only runs when it's needed, I
mean, that's a huge difference.

00:42:55.610 --> 00:42:57.620
It can be the difference
between something

00:42:57.620 --> 00:42:59.990
that you pay every month
for and something that

00:42:59.990 --> 00:43:02.120
maybe doesn't cost anything.

00:43:02.120 --> 00:43:07.515
Similarly, if people are used
to spinning up SQL databases

00:43:07.515 --> 00:43:09.140
and they have them
running all the time

00:43:09.140 --> 00:43:10.610
and they don't know
about BigQuery,

00:43:10.610 --> 00:43:13.520
and that they might be able to
store more data in BigQuery,

00:43:13.520 --> 00:43:15.890
get better performance,
and at lower cost,

00:43:15.890 --> 00:43:17.060
I think that's huge, too.

00:43:17.060 --> 00:43:18.590
So we really need
to educate people

00:43:18.590 --> 00:43:24.360
on the features of the platform
and where the cost savings are.

00:43:24.360 --> 00:43:27.890
And then I would also
mention about labels.

00:43:27.890 --> 00:43:29.720
We've done a lot with labels.

00:43:29.720 --> 00:43:33.740
We started out just using
labels to do what we do now

00:43:33.740 --> 00:43:36.110
with billing accounts,
to know which cost object

00:43:36.110 --> 00:43:38.210
a certain thing would bill to.

00:43:38.210 --> 00:43:41.220
And then the next
level of that was,

00:43:41.220 --> 00:43:44.480
how does my dev environment
cost differently than my prod

00:43:44.480 --> 00:43:47.240
environment, which labels
are very useful for.

00:43:47.240 --> 00:43:51.200
But in our environment, a lot
of our analysis in the cloud

00:43:51.200 --> 00:43:53.450
runs in a workflow
format, where you

00:43:53.450 --> 00:43:55.280
have many steps of
a workflow occurring

00:43:55.280 --> 00:43:57.210
to get a single job done.

00:43:57.210 --> 00:43:59.420
And we are actually
able to put labels

00:43:59.420 --> 00:44:01.650
on each of the
steps of a workflow,

00:44:01.650 --> 00:44:05.150
so that even though you might
know the cost of that analysis,

00:44:05.150 --> 00:44:08.400
you also know the cost of
each step of the analysis.

00:44:08.400 --> 00:44:11.360
So the team that's developing
that particular part,

00:44:11.360 --> 00:44:14.030
if their thing is the most
expensive part of the process,

00:44:14.030 --> 00:44:16.160
they're very
incentivized to improve

00:44:16.160 --> 00:44:19.460
the performance of their
steps so that it reduces cost.

00:44:19.460 --> 00:44:22.970
And the labels, let that be
very, very visible so everyone

00:44:22.970 --> 00:44:24.807
can see it and take action.

00:44:24.807 --> 00:44:25.640
MATT LEONARD: Great.

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:27.050
This is great recommendations.

00:44:27.050 --> 00:44:29.100
It sounds like, essentially,
driving visibility

00:44:29.100 --> 00:44:30.377
and accountability is key.

00:44:30.377 --> 00:44:32.210
Sounds like there's a
little bit of tooling,

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:34.043
a little bit of pricing
optimization, that's

00:44:34.043 --> 00:44:37.280
available through committed
use discounts, which is great.

00:44:37.280 --> 00:44:42.170
How many of you are using GKE
in order to manage your cost?

00:44:42.170 --> 00:44:43.310
Anyone?

00:44:43.310 --> 00:44:44.630
All right, Dale, take this one.

00:44:44.630 --> 00:44:46.338
So it's one of the
questions that we got,

00:44:46.338 --> 00:44:51.650
is if I am using GKE, how do
I organize my costs in order

00:44:51.650 --> 00:44:54.890
to understand to be able
to charge back by namespace

00:44:54.890 --> 00:44:56.860
those costs to the
internal departments?

00:44:56.860 --> 00:44:59.120
DALE HOPKINS: Yeah, so
for a background here,

00:44:59.120 --> 00:45:01.580
Lukas talks about
having 25,000 projects.

00:45:01.580 --> 00:45:04.970
I have one project that accounts
for over 70% of my billing.

00:45:04.970 --> 00:45:07.132
So actually diving
into that project

00:45:07.132 --> 00:45:08.840
and understanding a
lot more about what's

00:45:08.840 --> 00:45:11.277
happening in that project
is really important to me.

00:45:11.277 --> 00:45:12.860
And a significant
portion of that cost

00:45:12.860 --> 00:45:14.180
comes into Kubernetes.

00:45:14.180 --> 00:45:17.540
And so having the billing
aware, Kubernetes aware,

00:45:17.540 --> 00:45:19.490
because things like
namespaces are actually

00:45:19.490 --> 00:45:20.990
a really relevant
thing, in terms

00:45:20.990 --> 00:45:22.770
of breaking down those costs.

00:45:22.770 --> 00:45:24.710
And then actually
monitoring those costs

00:45:24.710 --> 00:45:26.530
are important, because
people may find--

00:45:26.530 --> 00:45:29.210
I mean, we talked about
that their dev environment,

00:45:29.210 --> 00:45:32.420
or their demo environment is
absolutely crushing something

00:45:32.420 --> 00:45:34.520
and they forgot that
they're not using that.

00:45:34.520 --> 00:45:36.415
And those are important
to break down,

00:45:36.415 --> 00:45:37.790
especially an
environment where--

00:45:37.790 --> 00:45:39.830
I mean, we're a
modestly sized company

00:45:39.830 --> 00:45:42.320
and we've got well over
200 microservices running

00:45:42.320 --> 00:45:44.420
at any one time in our
Kubernetes cluster.

00:45:44.420 --> 00:45:47.360
And so all it takes is
for one of those services

00:45:47.360 --> 00:45:48.240
to be out of whack.

00:45:48.240 --> 00:45:51.832
And it maybe doesn't present
itself at the project level,

00:45:51.832 --> 00:45:53.540
but if you were looking
each one of them,

00:45:53.540 --> 00:45:54.690
you can actually see that.

00:45:54.690 --> 00:45:57.290
So that's a very helpful thing
that we've been looking at.

00:45:57.290 --> 00:45:57.680
MATT LEONARD: Excellent.

00:45:57.680 --> 00:45:59.550
And I apologize, we are
running out of time.

00:45:59.550 --> 00:46:01.008
So some of the
other questions that

00:46:01.008 --> 00:46:03.470
have been asked in the Dory,
we'll try to answer offline.

00:46:03.470 --> 00:46:05.428
Just really quickly,
Dale, any closing thoughts

00:46:05.428 --> 00:46:06.790
for the audience?

00:46:06.790 --> 00:46:07.870
Any closing thoughts?

00:46:07.870 --> 00:46:09.870
DALE HOPKINS: I just
thought of one other thing.

00:46:09.870 --> 00:46:11.787
In terms of a show of
hands, does anybody here

00:46:11.787 --> 00:46:14.170
use Spanner or Bigtable?

00:46:14.170 --> 00:46:14.670
OK.

00:46:14.670 --> 00:46:16.180
We don't have a ton
of people, but I

00:46:16.180 --> 00:46:18.220
mean, interestingly
enough, I think

00:46:18.220 --> 00:46:22.090
it's Spotify that put out an
open source tool for actually

00:46:22.090 --> 00:46:24.070
scaling up and down Bigtable.

00:46:24.070 --> 00:46:26.830
And we actually modified
that to work for Spanner,

00:46:26.830 --> 00:46:28.640
and that made a
significant difference.

00:46:28.640 --> 00:46:31.645
So I think making costs
public and looking

00:46:31.645 --> 00:46:33.520
for these kind of things,
because autoscaling

00:46:33.520 --> 00:46:35.863
is one of the things that
really does reduce costs.

00:46:35.863 --> 00:46:37.780
I means, it's the promise
of the cloud, right?

00:46:37.780 --> 00:46:41.290
So my thoughts, make your
costs visible and, I guess,

00:46:41.290 --> 00:46:45.190
just look at always
working on it.

00:46:45.190 --> 00:46:47.530
CHRIS HOOVER: Maybe one
other slightly tactical thing

00:46:47.530 --> 00:46:48.300
that we haven't talked about.

00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:50.300
We didn't really talk too
much about networking.

00:46:50.300 --> 00:46:53.260
It would be worthwhile to
look at how much your data's

00:46:53.260 --> 00:46:56.560
spending within a zone,
but also between zones

00:46:56.560 --> 00:46:57.620
and across regions.

00:46:57.620 --> 00:46:59.300
Some of that might
be unintentional.

00:46:59.300 --> 00:47:01.180
You might be charging
a little bit more

00:47:01.180 --> 00:47:03.920
than you expect to there and
may not be getting a benefit.

00:47:03.920 --> 00:47:06.940
So just looking at where your
data is traveling across,

00:47:06.940 --> 00:47:10.000
systems and regions,
could be worthwhile.

00:47:10.000 --> 00:47:10.980
SHASHANK JOSHI: Yeah.

00:47:10.980 --> 00:47:13.210
I would like to leave one
point of understanding

00:47:13.210 --> 00:47:15.440
the importance of cost
management solution in place.

00:47:15.440 --> 00:47:17.840
So if you're not already
thinking about it,

00:47:17.840 --> 00:47:19.240
I would want to reiterate.

00:47:19.240 --> 00:47:24.910
And another thing, in terms
of we talked about tooling.

00:47:24.910 --> 00:47:28.360
In many organizations, you don't
have the choice of coming up

00:47:28.360 --> 00:47:29.780
with, for example, Data Studio.

00:47:29.780 --> 00:47:31.450
We looked at that
many customers.

00:47:31.450 --> 00:47:33.930
But many organizations
already have a tooling

00:47:33.930 --> 00:47:37.430
for cost management, because
GCP is not their first cost

00:47:37.430 --> 00:47:37.930
component.

00:47:37.930 --> 00:47:39.972
They may have other clouds,
private cloud, co-lo,

00:47:39.972 --> 00:47:41.420
all of those things.

00:47:41.420 --> 00:47:43.750
So you may not have a
choice, in terms of tooling,

00:47:43.750 --> 00:47:45.940
but make that choice early on.

00:47:45.940 --> 00:47:47.860
Because one example,
one experience we had,

00:47:47.860 --> 00:47:51.010
was one of the
retailers, we started off

00:47:51.010 --> 00:47:55.332
with the on-prem data storage
using Lstack and Tableau.

00:47:55.332 --> 00:47:57.040
Nothing wrong with
that platform, per se,

00:47:57.040 --> 00:48:00.730
but the system was
so slow, in terms

00:48:00.730 --> 00:48:03.520
of adapting to
Agile methodology,

00:48:03.520 --> 00:48:06.190
that we had to switch
back to BigQuery,

00:48:06.190 --> 00:48:08.590
and then Tableau in GCP.

00:48:08.590 --> 00:48:11.470
So make the tooling
decision also very early on,

00:48:11.470 --> 00:48:13.430
thinking not just from
today's requirements,

00:48:13.430 --> 00:48:16.120
but what you would
do in coming days.

00:48:16.120 --> 00:48:19.140
And lastly, I would say
automate for optimization,

00:48:19.140 --> 00:48:21.610
that I forgot to
mention earlier.

00:48:21.610 --> 00:48:25.090
That for cost reduction,
we came up with many ideas

00:48:25.090 --> 00:48:26.530
for automation.

00:48:26.530 --> 00:48:28.510
You can create
environments, like

00:48:28.510 --> 00:48:31.097
sandbox, timebox
environments, this environment

00:48:31.097 --> 00:48:32.680
is [INAUDIBLE] to
the dev team, but it

00:48:32.680 --> 00:48:35.650
will be automatically recycled
in two weeks or three weeks.

00:48:35.650 --> 00:48:38.110
So time [INAUDIBLE]
environments are--

00:48:38.110 --> 00:48:43.900
For some of the dev teams, which
are using a lot of cost, what

00:48:43.900 --> 00:48:45.820
we did for them was
all the dev at once,

00:48:45.820 --> 00:48:49.270
all the product labels labeled
as dev would be shut down.

00:48:49.270 --> 00:48:52.610
All the instances would be
shut down over the weekend,

00:48:52.610 --> 00:48:55.130
or out of the office hours.

00:48:55.130 --> 00:48:57.220
So you can use automation
to really optimize

00:48:57.220 --> 00:48:58.330
the cost using labels.

00:48:58.330 --> 00:48:59.100
MATT LEONARD: All right.

00:48:59.100 --> 00:49:00.808
And, Lukas, I'll ask
you to keep it fast,

00:49:00.808 --> 00:49:02.242
because we're
running out of time.

00:49:02.242 --> 00:49:03.700
LUKAS KARLSSON:
Well, I talk mostly

00:49:03.700 --> 00:49:07.060
about how to do things at
scale, so I'll actually just

00:49:07.060 --> 00:49:10.810
give a hint to people who have
a very simple billing setup.

00:49:10.810 --> 00:49:13.460
Google Bill's on
the calendar month.

00:49:13.460 --> 00:49:16.330
So if you set up a budget
that will alert you,

00:49:16.330 --> 00:49:19.960
let's say, at 50%, and
if you receive the alert

00:49:19.960 --> 00:49:23.690
from that budget and it's
before the 15th of the month,

00:49:23.690 --> 00:49:24.920
then that's problematic.

00:49:24.920 --> 00:49:26.690
And if it's after the
15th of the month,

00:49:26.690 --> 00:49:29.353
you don't need to
worry about it.

00:49:29.353 --> 00:49:30.770
MATT LEONARD:
That's a good point.

00:49:30.770 --> 00:49:31.570
Thank you, Lukas.

00:49:31.570 --> 00:49:32.530
Just closing thoughts.

00:49:32.530 --> 00:49:34.240
One of the questions is,
where can I learn more

00:49:34.240 --> 00:49:35.157
about cost management?

00:49:35.157 --> 00:49:37.260
We have a bunch of sessions
that you can attend.

00:49:37.260 --> 00:49:39.640
There's, in fact, 12 sessions
throughout Google Next

00:49:39.640 --> 00:49:40.900
this year.

00:49:40.900 --> 00:49:42.872
I would recommend you
take that opportunity

00:49:42.872 --> 00:49:44.580
to check in some of
those other sessions.

00:49:44.580 --> 00:49:47.530
They're going to teach you
about things like Data Studio

00:49:47.530 --> 00:49:49.980
and how to set budgets
for your environment.

00:49:49.980 --> 00:49:51.680
So please take that opportunity.

00:49:51.680 --> 00:49:53.290
We also have a bunch
of other material

00:49:53.290 --> 00:49:55.810
on our website that will
point you towards webinars,

00:49:55.810 --> 00:49:57.640
and other things, where you
can learn about cost management

00:49:57.640 --> 00:49:58.270
tooling.

00:49:58.270 --> 00:49:59.470
Thank you all for your time.

00:49:59.470 --> 00:50:00.370
